Reject ISO9660 directories whose extent, rounded up to whole logical blocks, does not fit in a
size_t.

read_children() rounds the 32-bit directory size up to logical blocks and keeps the result in a
size_t. The PVD checks accept any non-zero 16-bit logical block size; with one that is not a
power of two (e.g. 2080) and a directory size of 0xFFFFFFFF, the rounded value exceeds 4 GiB and
a 32-bit size_t truncates it to 1824 bytes, less than one block. The block loop then subtracts a
whole block, the step wraps around, and the loop walks past the read-ahead buffer until it hits a
byte that does not parse (in the wasm32 harnesses: heap memory past the input; a trap when the
input ends at the end of linear memory). 64-bit builds fail the oversized read-ahead instead.
Includes test_read_format_iso_dir_size_overflow, which can only fail on 32-bit hosts.
Not fixed upstream as of 432ab464 (no related issue or PR on 2026-11-21).
Upstream: none found (libarchive/libarchive issues and PRs searched for iso9660 read_children, directory size, SIZE_MAX, 32-bit).

